    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d232-f1" n="§" xml:id="d232-f1-content">
                <p>India has been organizing a Territorial Army, Its ultimate size is programmed for
                    130,000, but thus far India has had only limited success in recruiting men for
                    this force. The mission of the Territorial Army is to serve, not as a combat
                    force, but as an internal police force in time of war. [Footnote in the source
                    text.]</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d232-f2" n="||" xml:id="d232-f2-content">
                <p>Pakistan has a part-time National Guard, unarmed, with a personnel strength of
                    about 47,000. (Seven battalions, totalling about 6,700 men, were recently called
                    to active duty.) [Footnote in the source text.]</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d232-f3" n="¶" xml:id="d232-f3-content">
                <p>According to their respective tables of organization, Indian divisions consist of
                    about 21,500 men and Pakistani divisions of about 18,000 men. Brigades of both
                    countries consist of about 3,000 men. However, Indian units are very elastic and
                    are often considerably over strength. [Footnote in the source text.]</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d232-f4" n="**" xml:id="d232-f4-content">
                <p>Kashmiri battalions in the Indian Army are believed to be at full strength, i.e.
                    1,000 men per unit, but they are probably inferior in quality to the regular
                    Indian units. Although the Kashmiri battalions in the Pakistani Army contain
                    only about 650 men each, they are nearly as good in quality as the regular
                    Pakistani units. [Footnote in the source text.]</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
